  card="The ability of a lens to converge or diverge light rays depends on its focal length: a convex lens of short focal length bends rays through large angles, and a concave lens of very short focal length causes higher divergence. This degree of convergence or divergence is expressed as the power of the lens, the reciprocal of its focal length: P = 1/f. The SI unit of power is the dioptre, D; if f is in metres, power is in dioptres, so 1 dioptre is the power of a lens of focal length 1 metre. The power of a convex lens is positive and of a concave lens negative. A prescription of +2.0 D means a convex lens of focal length +0.50 m, and -2.5 D a concave lens of focal length -0.40 m. The net power of lenses in contact is the algebraic sum of their individual powers.",
